    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Rentals</span>
    <h1>Hanoi Scooter Rental: Automatic Scooter Checks and Mistakes in Rain</h1>
    <p>Rain riding in Hanoi is a technique problem, not a cancellation problem, and the technique starts at pickup rather than at the first drop. The checks that matter in the wet are quick and unforgiving in the best way: tyres, brakes, lights, and the poncho under the seat. Verified rates keep the decision-making calm, from the Honda Click and Yamaha Mio at 150,000 VND per day to the Honda Vision, Air Blade, and electric scooter at 200,000 VND per day, and the counter at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, open 09:00 to 21:00 daily, runs every check under a dry roof before you commit.</p>
    <p>This page pairs with the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/automatic-scooter-in-rain-selection-guide/">automatic scooter selection guide for rain</a>, which chooses the machine for the season; the checks below make the actual unit prove itself on a wet afternoon. For rates across tiers, the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost overview</a> holds every verified number, and the <a href="/faq/">rental FAQ</a> covers the paperwork. The mistakes in these sections cost nothing to avoid, which is the only kind of mistake worth reading about.</p>
    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents"><ol>
<li><a href="#sec-1">What wet streets change about riding</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-2">The five-minute wet-weather pass at pickup</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-3">Braking technique for painted lines and metal</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-4">The poncho routine and what lives under the seat</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-5">Mistake: riding through the first minutes of a downpour</a></li>
<li><a href="#sec-6">Mistake: treating standing water as shallow</a></li>
